+Bots are `User` records with `role: :bot`. They're members of rooms like people, but they
+never sign in. They post through an HTTP API instead. Unlike people, bots do **not** join
+open rooms by default — see [Room membership](#room-membership).
 
 ## Model (`User::Bot`)
 
@@ -28,6 +27,40 @@ through an HTTP API instead.
 - Pages send `Cache-Control: no-store` since they show credentials. No pagination.
 - Form params use the `user` key (`form_with model: @bot`).
 
+## Room membership
+
+- People join every open room (`User#grant_membership_to_open_rooms`, `unless: :bot?`,
+  and `Rooms::Open#grant_access_to_all_users`). Bots only do when
+  `users.joins_all_open_rooms` is on ("Add to all open rooms" switch in the bot form,
+  off by default). `User.joining_open_rooms` = people + such bots; use it wherever
+  "everyone joins open rooms" logic lives.
+- Turning the switch on (`User::Bot#join_all_open_rooms`, `after_save_commit` on the
+  change to `true`) grants every existing open room; rooms that become open later
+  (created, or converted from closed) pick the bot up in `Rooms::Open`. Turning it off
+  doesn't touch memberships.
+- People add bots by hand: closed rooms through the usual `user_ids[]` switches; open
+  rooms through `bot_ids[]` switches ("Give <bot> access to this room") rendered for bots
+  without the flag in `rooms/opens/_user`. `Rooms::OpensController` revises only bot
+  memberships and only when `bot_ids` is submitted (the form always sends a blank
+  `bot_ids[]`), so a rename-only request never drops bots. Bots with the flag show a
+  plain check (they can't be removed from open rooms while it's on).
+
+### `joined` event
+
+**All** membership creation goes through `room.memberships.grant_to` (also `revise`,
+`Room.create_for`, `Rooms::Open`, `join_all_open_rooms`). It uses
+`insert_all(..., returning: :user_id)`, so only *newly inserted* memberships come back
+(the unique index on `room_id, user_id` drops duplicates) → `Room#deliver_joined_webhooks_later`
+enqueues `Webhook::JoinedDeliveryJob` for those bots with webhooks. That's what makes it
+"exactly once per room" (re-granting, re-submitting forms, toggling the switch off/on don't
+re-announce). A bot that's removed and later re-added is announced again.
+
+- Never for direct rooms (`room.direct?`).
+- Enqueued in `ActiveRecord.after_all_transactions_commit` (the app has
+  `enqueue_after_transaction_commit` off), so a rolled-back grant never announces.
+- `Webhook#deliver_joined` skips the POST if the bot's no longer a member when the job runs.
+- Don't add memberships with a bare `Membership.insert_all` for bots — it bypasses `joined`.
+
 ## API (`Messages::ByBotsController`)
 
 `POST /rooms/:room_id/:bot_key/messages` (`room_bot_messages_path`)
@@ -81,14 +114,23 @@ doesn't filter by role) — keep it that way.
 }
 ```
 
-- `event` is always `"mention"` for now; add new event types alongside it.
+- `event` is `"mention"` here; see `joined` below. Add new event types alongside them
+  (`Webhook#*_payload_for`, sharing `room_payload_for`).
 - `body.text` = `plain_text_body` with the bot's own `@Name` (its
   `attachable_plain_text_representation`) removed and the result trimmed. Other mentions stay.
 - `body.html` = the stored rich-text HTML (`message.body.body.to_html`).
 - `reply_to` = `room_bot_messages_path(room, bot.bot_key)` (the bot's own posting URL),
   `link` = `room_at_message_path`. Paths are relative on purpose.
 
-### Response handling (`Webhook#deliver`)
+`joined` (bot added to a non-direct room):
+
+```json
+{ "event": "joined", "room": { "id": 5, "name": "Ops", "reply_to": "/rooms/5/<bot_key>/messages" } }
+```
+
+### Response handling (`Webhook#deliver`, `Webhook#deliver_joined`)
+
+Same for every event (`Webhook#deliver_to`): the reply is posted into the event's room.
 
 - `200` + `text/plain` → escaped like the posting API (newlines → `<br>`) and posted as the bot.
 - `200` + `text/html` → posted as rich text (Action Text sanitizes on render).
